####################################################################################################
# End-to-end walkthrough of the API, in dependency order.
#
# Prerequisites
#   docker compose up -d db mailpit
#   dotnet run --project Src/Presentation/AppTemplate.Api
#
# Run the requests top to bottom. Every value a later request needs is captured from an earlier
# response, except the email confirmation token: it arrives by mail, and this file format has no way
# to pull a substring out of a message body. Request 4 fetches the mail so you can read the token;
# paste it into @confirmationToken and carry on. That step is marked MANUAL below.
#
# Works in the VS Code REST Client extension and in Visual Studio's .http editor. Both resolve
# {{requestName.response.body.$.field}} against a named earlier response.
####################################################################################################

@host = http://localhost:5187
@api = {{host}}/api/v1
@mailpit = http://localhost:8025

# Password satisfies appsettings.Development.json: >= 8 chars, upper, lower, digit, non-alphanumeric.
@userName = ada
@email = ada@localhost
@password = Str0ng!Passw0rd
@newPassword = {{password}}-2
@resetPassword = {{password}}-3

###
# 1. Register. The account is committed before the mail is handed to the relay, so
#    confirmationEmailSent=false means "use the resend endpoint", not "sign-up failed".
# @name register
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userName": "{{userName}}",
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# 2. Registering the same address again is a 409 with a stable machine-readable `code`,
#    not a 500. Expected to fail — it is here to show the shape of a ProblemDetails body.
POST {{api}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "userName": "{{userName}}",
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# 3. Logging in before confirmation is refused: Identity:RequireConfirmedEmail is true.
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# 4. MANUAL STEP. Read the confirmation mail out of mailpit and copy the `token` query
#    parameter of the confirm-email link into @confirmationToken below.
#    The browser UI at http://localhost:8025 shows the same message.
# @name confirmationMail
GET {{mailpit}}/api/v1/message/latest
Accept: application/json

###
# Paste the token from request 4 here.
@confirmationToken = paste-the-token-from-request-4

###
# 5. Confirm the address. POST with a JSON body, deliberately not GET with a query string:
#    a single-use token must not land in access logs, browser history or a Referer header.
POST {{api}}/auth/confirm-email
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "token": "{{confirmationToken}}"
}

###
# 6. Resend the confirmation mail. Answers 204 even for an address that does not exist,
#    so the endpoint cannot be used to enumerate accounts.
POST {{api}}/auth/resend-confirmation-email
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}"
}

###
# 7. Log in. Captures the access token and the refresh token for everything below.
# @name login
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
@accessToken = {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}}
@refreshToken = {{login.response.body.$.refreshToken}}

###
# 8. Without a token: 401. The application's fallback policy requires an authenticated user,
#    so an endpoint is protected unless it opts out.
GET {{api}}/todo-lists

###
# 9. Create a list. 201 with a Location header pointing at request 11.
# @name createList
POST {{api}}/todo-lists
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Groceries"
}

###
@todoListId = {{createList.response.body.$.id}}

###
# 10. Add an item, with tags.
# @name addItem
POST {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}/items
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Buy milk",
  "description": "Semi-skimmed, two litres",
  "tags": ["errand", "dairy"]
}

###
@todoItemId = {{addItem.response.body.$.id}}

###
# 11. Read the list back. The item added above is present, with its audit stamps. The response
#     carries a strong, quoted ETag: the version this list is at, opaque to the caller.
# @name readList
GET {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
@todoListETag = {{readList.response.headers.ETag}}

###
# 12. Read the single item. Its ETag is the list's — the list is the aggregate, so there is no
#     separate version for one item inside it.
GET {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}/items/{{todoItemId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 13. Rename the list conditionally, naming the version read in request 11. Applied because
#     nothing has changed it since: 204.
PUT {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
If-Match: {{todoListETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Groceries (this week)"
}

###
# 14. Replay request 13 with the same If-Match. The rename above already moved the list past that
#     version, so this one is refused: 412 with a ProblemDetails body carrying
#     "code": "precondition.failed". Read the list again to retry with the current ETag.
PUT {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
If-Match: {{todoListETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Too late"
}

###
# 15. Complete the item. No If-Match: Concurrency:IfMatch defaults to Optional, so an
#     unconditional write is still accepted — see docs/adr/0013. Raises a domain event, published
#     after the commit succeeds.
POST {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}/items/{{todoItemId}}/complete
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 16. Completing it twice is refused by the aggregate's invariant, surfaced as a 409.
POST {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}/items/{{todoItemId}}/complete
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 17. First page of the caller's lists. Only lists the caller owns are visible: ownership is a
#     filter inside the read query, so somebody else's list is 404, never 403.
GET {{api}}/todo-lists?page=1&pageSize=10
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 18. An out-of-range page size is a 400 with a ProblemDetails body, not a silent clamp.
GET {{api}}/todo-lists?page=1&pageSize=10000
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 19. Remove the item.
DELETE {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}/items/{{todoItemId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 20. Rotate the token pair. The presented refresh token is always revoked; replaying one that was
#     already used revokes the whole family for that user.
# @name refresh
POST {{api}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

###
@rotatedRefreshToken = {{refresh.response.body.$.refreshToken}}

###
# 21. Replay the token consumed by request 20: 401, and every refresh token in that family is now
#     revoked. Run request 20 again afterwards to see the rotated one rejected too.
POST {{api}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

###
# 22. Delete the list. Cascades to its items. No If-Match, accepted for the same reason as request 15.
DELETE {{api}}/todo-lists/{{todoListId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

###
# 23. Log out, revoking the refresh token from request 20.
POST {{api}}/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{rotatedRefreshToken}}"
}

###
# 24. Liveness. No dependency is checked, so an orchestrator does not restart the API because the
#     database blinked. Anonymous by design.
GET {{host}}/health

###
# 25. Readiness. Checks the database, and is what a load balancer should poll.
GET {{host}}/health/ready

###
# 26. Rate limiting. The auth policy allows 10 requests per minute per client address; run this
#     block repeatedly to get a 429 carrying Retry-After and a ProblemDetails body.
#     Behind a proxy this partitions correctly only when ReverseProxy:Enabled is configured —
#     otherwise every caller shares the proxy's single window. See SECURITY.md.
POST {{api}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@localhost",
  "password": "wrong-password"
}
